<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austro-Hungarian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Austria-Hungary, Dual Monarchy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austro-Hungarian Empire, or Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, was a dual monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> and ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> and his successor. It combined many nationalities, whose tensions strained its political system. After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> it went to war in 1914, and it dissolved in 1918 at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire.jpg" alt="Austro-Hungarian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Austria-Hungary_(1914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was the last phase in the constitutional evolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526 the Habsburgs assumed the Hungarian throne, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 all the former Hungarian territories had passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to them. Hungary rebelled against Habsburg rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakoczis-war-of-independence">Rákóczi's War of Independence</a> of 1703 to 1711 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution of 1848</a> to 1849, which the Austrian army crushed with Russian help, replacing Hungarian autonomy with absolutist rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a> of 1866 settled in favour of a Lesser German solution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> created the dual monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The state was a real union between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisleithania">Cisleithania</a>, the western and northern lands of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Transleithania</a>, the Kingdom of Hungary. The two halves were co-equal and shared one monarch, titled Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Apostolic King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. They conducted a common diplomatic, defence and monetary policy through common ministries of foreign affairs and defence and a finance ministry that paid for them. Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> was an autonomous region under the Hungarian crown, with its own settlement of 1868.</p><p>The economy, largely rural, slowly modernised after 1867. Railways opened remote areas, and by 1913 the combined track length reached 43,280 kilometres. The Austro-Hungarian Bank was established in 1878 with offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Vienna was a centre of European modernism, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a> and to composers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> under joint rule in 1878 and annexed it in October 1908, provoking the Bosnian crisis. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914 was followed by anti-Serb riots, and the authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> imprisoned or deported about 5,500 prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, of whom 700 to 2,200 died in prison. The empire declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 28 July 1914 and fought as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>.</p><p>By 1918 the economy had deteriorated and support for the war had gone. Strikes and army uprisings became common, and nationalist movements, encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, demanded separation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of Villa Giusti was signed on 3 November 1918, when the state had already effectively dissolved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> were treated as its successors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> were recognised as independent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austria-Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The administrative, legal and military institutions of this period, including the General Civil Code of 1811, passed to <b>Austria-Hungary</b> and, in part, to the successor states of central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First World War the Russian army first won victories against <b>Austria-Hungary</b>, but after the Gorlice–Tarnów offensive its Great Retreat of 1915 looted and abandoned the kingdom, and it deported hundreds of thousands of inhabitants suspected of collaborating with the enemy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Czechoslovakia was created as <b>Austria-Hungary</b> collapsed at the end of the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its central problem, how to govern many peoples with distinct laws and languages under one dynasty, passed to the Austrian Empire and <b>Austria-Hungary</b>, and still informs debates about federalism and nationality in Central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 October 1918, as <b>Austria-Hungary</b> collapsed, the Czechoslovak state was proclaimed in Prague, and the kingdom became the core of the new republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Austria-Hungary</b> dissolved at the end of the First World War, the Hungarian Democratic Republic and then the Hungarian Soviet Republic were proclaimed in 1918 and 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
